/*!

\page arcstk-verify

\brief Verify locally calculated checksums against AccurateRip reference checksums

\version @PROJECT_VERSION@



\section verify_syno SYNOPSIS

arcstk-verify [OPTIONS] AUDIOFILE1 AUDIOFILE2 ...


\section verify_desc DESCRIPTION

Calculates AccurateRip checksums from losslessly encoded audiofiles and table of
contents (ToC) information and verifies them against reference checksums.

\snippet{doc} include/text_calcdesc.dox TEXT_CALCDESC

Providing reference checksums is mandatory. They can be specified in one of
three ways: passed as a comma-separated list of hexadecimal values using the
\b --refvalues option, or as the name of a binary file (typically a
<tt>dBAR-*.bin</tt> file) using the \b -r option, or as binary content (as
provided from AccurateRip) via stdin.

The result will be the "best possible" match; this is the result of the
comparison that provided the least total number of non-identical checksums for
any index positions in the input. The total number of these "non-matches" in the
compared set is called "difference"; for example, if all but two checksums
match, the difference is 2 and all but the corresponding two files or tracks
that did not match can be considered verified.

The output declares locally calculated actual checksums as "Mine"; reference
checksums are called "Theirs". Each block of reference checksums in the input
corresponds to a "Theirs" column in the output; the "Theirs" columns are
numbered in the order, their corresponding blocks occurred in the input.

Matches of checksums will be marked with symbol "==" in the corresponding table
cell. For a non-matching checksum, the corresponding table cell will contain the
respective checksum. For ANSI terminals, colored output is optionally available.

Switch \b --print-all-matches prints every match attempt that was actually
performed, regardless of ARCSv2 or ARCSv1 or whether a match attempt was
successful.


\section verify_opts OPTIONS

\subsection verify_opts_in Options for input

\par \--no-album
Shorthand for \b --no-first \b --no-last; suppresses special treatment of the
first and last track. Default is OFF. This switch is intended for cases in which
only some tracks of an album are to be verified.

\par \--no-first
Do not treat the first of the input audiofiles as first track of an album.
Default is OFF. See also \b --no-last and \b --no-album.

\par \--no-last
Do not treat the last of the input audiofiles as last track of an album. Default
is OFF. See also \b --no-first and \b --no-album.

\snippet{doc} include/option_parser.dox    OPT_PARSER

\snippet{doc} include/option_reader.dox    OPT_READER

\par \--refvalues=0x111,0x222,0x333,...
Comma-separated list of hexadecimal values (with or without leading '0x' prefix)
to use as reference values for verification. If \b --refvalues is specified,
option \b --metafile is ignored and therefore ToC information and AccurateRip
url and id are not available. Consequently, options \b --print-id and
\b --print-url are without effect if \b --refvalues is specified.
If neither \b --response nor \b --refvalues is specified, the binary content as
provided by AccurateRip is expected on stdin.

\par -r, \--response=RESPONSEFILE
Specify the binary file with the AccurateRip response. Parsed text files will
not be accepted. If neither \b --response nor \b --refvalues is specified, the
binary content as provided by AccurateRip is expected on stdin.


\subsection verify_opts_out Options for output

\par -b, \--boolean
Return the difference of the best match. Hence, 0 indicates a successful
verification and some value bigger than 0 indicates that some input tracks
could not be verified. The result only informs whether some reference block
could be matched for either ARCSv2 or ARCSv1.

\snippet{doc} include/option_coldelim.dox          OPT_COLDELIM

\par \--colors, \--colors=[default|TYPE:COLOR+COLOR,TYPE:COLOR+COLOR,...]
Use colored output for match results when printing to the command line.
Note that output to files will contain ANSI escape sequences.
A semicolon-separated list of pairs of a TYPE and a color specification
separated by a colon ':' is accepted as an optional parameter.
\n\n
TYPE and COLOR values are case-insensitive. If a custom color list is provided,
any TYPE not explicitly assigned a color is rendered as FG_DEFAULT+BG_DEFAULT
regardless whether it has a different default color.
\n\n
To use custom colors provide a comma-separated list of \b TYPE:COLOR pairs. Each
pair maps a checksum display type to one or two colors.
\htmlonly
<table class="m-table">
	<tr> <th>TYPE</th>     <th>Definition</th>                            </tr>
	<tr> <td>MATCH</td>    <td>"Theirs" checksum matches "Mine"</td>      </tr>
	<tr> <td>MISMATCH</td> <td>"Theirs" checksum differs from "Mine"</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>MINE</td>     <td>Locally computed checksums</td>            </tr>
</table>
\endhtmlonly
\manonly
.sp
.TS
tab(|);
cl.
TYPE      | Definition
--------- | -----------
 MATCH    | "Theirs" checksum matches "Mine"
 MISMATCH | "Theirs" checksum differs from "Mine"
 MINE     | Locally computed checksums
.TE
.sp
\endmanonly
A color specification is either a single color name (foreground) or two names
separated by \b + (foreground + background) in any order.
Example: \b FG_GREEN+BG_DEFAULT
\htmlonly
<table class="m-table">
	<tr> <th>Attribute</th> <th>Possible Values</th> </tr>
	<tr> <td>TYPE</td>  <td>MATCH, MISMATCH, MINE</td> </tr>
	<tr> <td>COLOR</td>
		<td>FG_BLACK, FG_RED, FG_GREEN, FG_YELLOW, FG_BLUE,
			FG_MAGENTA, FG_CYAN, FG_WHITE, FG_DEFAULT,
			BG_BLACK, BG_RED, BG_GREEN, BG_YELLOW, BG_BLUE,
			BG_MAGENTA, BG_CYAN, BG_WHITE, BG_DEFAULT </td> </tr>
</table>
\endhtmlonly
\manonly
.sp
.TS
tab(|);
cl.
Attribute | Possible Values
--------- | ---------------
   TYPE   | MATCH, MISMATCH, MINE
   COLOR  | FG_BLACK, FG_RED, FG_GREEN, FG_YELLOW, FG_BLUE, FG_MAGENTA, FG_CYAN, FG_WHITE, FG_DEFAULT, BG_BLACK, BG_RED, BG_GREEN, BG_YELLOW, BG_BLUE, BG_MAGENTA, BG_CYAN, BG_WHITE, BG_DEFAULT
.TE
.sp
\endmanonly
The default behavior is \b --colors or \b --colors=default uses the following
predefined colors.
\htmlonly
<table class="m-table">
	<tr> <th>TYPE</th>     <th>Default color specification</th> </tr>
	<tr> <td>MATCH</td>    <td>FG_GREEN+BG_DEFAULT</td>   </tr>
	<tr> <td>MISMATCH</td> <td>FG_RED+BG_DEFAULT</td>     </tr>
	<tr> <td>MINE</td>     <td>FG_DEFAULT+BG_DEFAULT</td> </tr>
</table>
\endhtmlonly
\manonly
.sp
.TS
tab(|);
cl.
TYPE      | Default color specification
--------- | ---------------------------
 MATCH    | FG_GREEN+BG_DEFAULT
 MISMATCH | FG_RED+BG_DEFAULT
 MINE     | FG_DEFAULT+BG_DEFAULT
.TE
.sp
\endmanonly


\par \--confidence
Print the confidence value of each "Theirs" checksum in a column on the right
hand side of the respective "Theirs" column. This switch will be ignored if
\b --refvalues is used.

\snippet{doc} include/option_help.dox              OPT_HELP

\snippet{doc} include/option_listaudioformats.dox  OPT_LISTAUDIOFORMATS

\snippet{doc} include/option_listtocformats.dox    OPT_LISTTOCFORMATS

\snippet{doc} include/option_nofilenames.dox       OPT_NOFILENAMES
\snippet{doc} include/option_nolabels.dox          OPT_NOLABELS
\snippet{doc} include/option_nolengths.dox         OPT_NOLENGTHS
\snippet{doc} include/option_nooffsets.dox         OPT_NOOFFSETS

\par -n, \--no-output
Do not print any result. Implies \b --boolean, therefore the return value will
be informative.

\snippet{doc} include/option_notracknos.dox        OPT_NOTRACKNOS

\par \--print-all-matches
Print each match result for each reference block. If combined with
\b --refvalues, the effect is just to activate the output of the match result
for ARCSv1 as well as ARCSv2.

\par \--print-id
Print the AccurateRip id of the reference data, if available. This switch will
be ignored if \b --refvalues is used.

\par \--print-url
Print the AccurateRip url of the reference data, if available. This switch will
be ignored if \b --refvalues is used.

\snippet{doc} include/option_metafile.dox  OPT_METAFILE

\snippet{doc} include/option_logfile.dox   OPT_LOGFILE

\snippet{doc} include/option_outfile.dox   OPT_OUTFILE

\snippet{doc} include/option_quiet.dox     OPT_QUIET

\snippet{doc} include/option_verbosity.dox OPT_VERBOSITY

\snippet{doc} include/option_version.dox   OPT_VERSION


\section verify_exmp EXAMPLES

\subsection verify_ALBUMTOCM 1. Album + ToC, using the audio files specified in the ToC

You have: An album ripped to a single audio file, say fLaC, or a set of files
and the ToC (as Cuesheet). The Cuesheet specifies the actual audiofiles, may it
be a single file or one file per track. You saved the checksums from AccurateRip
in a local dBAR file (see man 1 arcstk-parse).

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin -m myalbum/toc.cue\endcode



\subsection verify_ALBUMTOCF 2. Album + ToC, overriding the files in the ToC, if any


\subsubsection verify_ALBUMTOCF_1 Single audio file

You have: An album ripped to a single audio file, say fLaC, and the ToC as
Cuesheet. The Cuesheet specifies audio files, but you want to override them with
\c myalbum/album_rip.flac instead. You saved the checksums from AccurateRip in a
local dBAR file (see man 1 arcstk-parse).

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin -m myalbum/toc.cue myalbum/album_rip.flac\endcode


\subsubsection verify_ALBUMTOCF_2 One audio file per track

You have: An album ripped one-file-per-track to a set of fLaC files, and the ToC
as a Cuesheet. The Cuesheet is stored elsewhere or references WAV files, so you
want to override its file references. You saved the checksums from AccurateRip
in a local dBAR file (see man 1 arcstk-parse).

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin -m myalbum/toc.cue myalbum/track_1.flac myalbum/track_2.flac ...\endcode



\subsection verify_ALBUMFILES 3. Audiofiles forming an album, but no ToC

You have: A set of audio files that you know to form an album, but there is no
ToC information. The filenames are such that the first filename is also the
first track and the last filename is the last track of the album. You saved the
checksums from AccurateRip in a local dBAR file (see man 1 arcstk-parse).

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin --album myalbum/f1.flac myalbum/f2.flac ...\endcode


\subsection verify_FILES 4. Some audiofiles but no ToC

You have: A set of audiofiles that do not form an album in the passed order,
e.g. the mapping from files to tracks is not known. You saved the checksums of
some possibly matching album from AccurateRip in a local dBAR file
(see man 1 arcstk-parse).

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin myalbum/track_1.flac myalbum/track_2.flac ...\endcode

If you know that \c track_1.flac is the first track, declare it accordingly:

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin --first myalbum/track_1.flac myalbum/track_2.flac ...\endcode

If you know that \c track_15.flac is the last track, declare it accordingly:

\code{.sh}$ arcstk-verify -r dBAR-some_id.bin --last myalbum/track_1.flac ... myalbum/track_15.flac\endcode

\b NOTE The number of audiofiles passed must be equal to the track count of the
album.


